 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
+### §check-fails-closed — an internal failure aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command inside the check aborts it with a non-zero exit. Large staged
+  files still only warn and end in `exit 0`.
+- Forbidden: dropping `-e`, or swallowing internal errors so the script exits 0 after a failure.
+- Reason: maintainer decision. Running fail-open meant breakage inside the check went unnoticed.
+- Caveat: under `-e` with `pipefail`, a `[ test ] && cmd` as the last command of the `while` body fails the whole
+  pipeline whenever the test is false, which blocks ordinary commits. Use `if … then … fi` there. Staged-then-deleted
+  files (the 2026-09-10 cause) are skipped by `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` before `wc` runs.
+- Supersedes: §check-fails-open.
+
+### §check-fails-open — superseded 2026-10-22 by §check-fails-closed
 - Since: 2026-09-11
 - Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
 - Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
